-- title:
--   Invertible module over a semilocalisation descends to r-inverting algebras
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring, $A$ a scheme and $f : A \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ a morphism satisfying `AbelianSchemePropertyBundle`, i.e. $f$ is smooth and proper, each fibre $f^{-1}(s)$ over a point $s$ of $\operatorname{Spec} S$ is connected, and $f$ carries a relative group law (a functorial group structure on the sets of sections over $\operatorname{Spec} S$, compatible with base change). Let $\mathfrak p$ be a prime of $S$, let $C$ be an $S$-algebra and let $C_0$ be an $S$-algebra and $C$-algebra, compatibly, which is a localisation of $C$ at the submonoid generated by the image of $S \setminus \mathfrak p$. Let $\mathcal M_0$ be a module on $A_{C_0} = A \times_{\operatorname{Spec} S} \operatorname{Spec} C_0$ which is invertible, in the sense that every point of $A_{C_0}$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ on which the restriction of $\mathcal M_0$ is isomorphic to the unit module of $U$. Then there is $r \in S$ with $r \notin \mathfrak p$ such that for every $S$-algebra and $C$-algebra $X$ (compatibly) in which the image of $r$ is a unit, every ring homomorphism $\varphi : X \to C_0$ of $C$-algebras, and every morphism $\kappa : A_{C_0} \to A_X$ commuting with the projections to $A$ and covering $\operatorname{Spec} \varphi$ on the second factors, there is an invertible module $\mathcal M$ on $A_X$ together with an isomorphism $\kappa^{*}\mathcal M \cong \mathcal M_0$.
--
--   This is the spreading-out (limit) step for invertible modules on an abelian scheme: a line bundle defined after semilocalising the base at $\mathfrak p$ already comes, by pullback, from any algebra in which one suitable $r \notin \mathfrak p$ has been inverted, the statement being formulated uniformly in the target algebra $X$ and the comparison morphism $\kappa$. It feeds the corresponding spreading-out statement for symmetric rigidified line bundles used in the construction of polarisations on relative Picard schemes.
